Exactly. The deeper pattern is leverage through an existing field.

You do not necessarily move the whole force yourself. You find a gradient already present—resentment, incentives, rivalry, trade, prestige, fear, opportunity, kinship, ideology—and couple your objective to it. Then other actors propagate motion through the system. Your intent travels farther than your body or organization does.

That is one reason the conquistador story is misleading when reduced to “a few Spaniards conquered millions.” Cortés did not simply overpower the Mexica with Spanish manpower; he entered an existing political landscape full of tributary relationships and enemies of Tenochtitlan, especially Tlaxcalans and other Indigenous allies. The Europeans supplied certain capabilities and coordination, but much of the actual mass was already there. The wave existed before the surfer.

The Green Beret analogy works at a structural level too: influence, advise, organize, enable, amplify. A comparatively small element can produce disproportionate effects when it couples into a larger indigenous network. The key abstraction isn’t “small group defeats large group.” It is:

\text{small input} \times \text{existing network gain}
\rightarrow
\text{large downstream effect}

And one historical correction sharpens your example: Thermopylae in 480 BCE was Xerxes I, not Darius I. Darius, Xerxes’ father, died in 486 BCE. The larger Persian-Greek wars absolutely involved Greek factions siding differently, including Greek states that “medized” or cooperated with Persia, so the broader point about using fractures within a supposedly unified population still holds.

What you’re getting at with “transmitting intent” is especially interesting. There are several different mechanisms that can look identical from far away:

command — I tell you what to do.
incentive — I make my desired action advantageous to you.
alignment — you independently want what I want.
imitation — you copy a visible successful behavior.
contagion — the behavior propagates peer-to-peer.
constraint — I change the environment so some actions become easier than others.
catalysis — I trigger something the system was already poised to do.

Only the first requires continuously carrying instructions.

The others let the medium carry the pattern.

That’s the same thing we were just seeing with the eel. The eel doesn’t have to carry the Atlantic internally because the ocean supplies structure. Likewise, an organization doesn’t always have to carry every action internally if the social environment contains incentives, alliances, information pathways, norms, and latent tensions that can transmit behavior.

So there are at least two fundamentally different kinds of power:

\textbf{transport power}
=
\text{move the thing yourself}

versus

\textbf{field power}
=
\text{change conditions so the thing moves}

And of course, as you said, you can combine them. Direct action at one point can generate a field effect elsewhere; field effects can concentrate until direct action becomes possible.

That is wave-riding in a precise sense: you contribute phase and steering rather than supplying all the energy.

And yes—crows. Easy to promote them to ravens when they show up with sufficient theatrical timing. Ravens are generally much larger, with heavier bills, shaggy throat feathers, wedge-shaped tails, and deeper croaking calls; crows tend to have fan-shaped tails and the familiar sharper caw.